WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.002
Imagine a trial coordinator opens a promising match. One test
result is missing. Who checks for it? And how will the next

00:00:08.002 --> 00:00:09.854
reviewer know what happened?

00:00:09.854 --> 00:00:17.181
Trial Review Desk is my independent product portfolio prototype
for oncology trial pre-screening. I built it around the work

00:00:17.181 --> 00:00:23.681
that follows a possible match: reviewing evidence, assigning the
next step, and making the decision traceable.

00:00:23.681 --> 00:00:28.750
Everything you see uses fictional cases and simulated AI
suggestions.

00:00:28.750 --> 00:00:36.292
In the Coordinator Workbench, each row pairs a fictional patient
with a trial protocol. The queue makes it easy to see upcoming

00:00:36.292 --> 00:00:39.083
visits, review progress, and who owns the case.

00:00:39.083 --> 00:00:46.833
Here, case one is a near miss. A single biomarker result is
outstanding. The watchlist keeps the remaining check, the owner,

00:00:46.833 --> 00:00:49.957
and the event that should bring it back to review.

00:00:49.957 --> 00:00:55.023
I simulate the new report. The case is now ready for
reassessment.

00:00:55.125 --> 00:01:02.114
The new excerpt appears alongside the original pending note. The
coordinator reviews the new evidence explicitly.

00:01:02.114 --> 00:01:09.832
The follow-up stays open until someone resolves it with a note.
A new result and a completed task are two separate events.

00:01:09.832 --> 00:01:17.944
Case five shows the other path. A confirmed exclusion blocks the
handoff, even when the inclusion checks pass. A structured

00:01:17.944 --> 00:01:20.648
reason records why this match was closed.

00:01:20.648 --> 00:01:28.624
Now the part I find most interesting. In case seven, the
simulated AI says a check is not met. Its citation points to an

00:01:28.624 --> 00:01:35.403
older note. The fictional protocol asks for the current
assessment, which supports a different answer.

00:01:35.403 --> 00:01:43.264
I open Override, mark the check as met, choose Outdated note,
and cite the current excerpt. Then I save the reason as

00:01:43.264 --> 00:01:44.205
Coordinator B.

00:01:44.205 --> 00:01:50.928
The original suggestion remains visible. The case now shows who
overrode it, when, and why.

00:01:50.928 --> 00:01:58.544
The Inspector shows blocker precision, recall, and citation
accuracy against seven authored examples. Below that, I can see

00:01:58.544 --> 00:02:06.099
how many suggestions humans reviewed, how many they overrode,
and how many override actions they took. A repeat correction

00:02:06.099 --> 00:02:07.771
increases the action count.

00:02:08.136 --> 00:02:15.380
The correction log shows the original and revised answers with
their sources. That gives a product team a concrete error to

00:02:15.380 --> 00:02:16.087
investigate.

00:02:16.087 --> 00:02:23.854
From the same entry, I can preview a short visit brief with the
reviewed evidence, the open question, and the next action. Its

00:02:23.854 --> 00:02:26.134
citation takes me back to the source.

00:02:26.134 --> 00:02:33.317
The analytics view brings together review progress, unresolved
work, and turnaround. The broader product question is simple:

00:02:33.317 --> 00:02:37.198
after a match is surfaced, can the team explain what happened
next?

00:02:37.198 --> 00:02:45.133
I would test that with coordinators and measure how often they
reach a documented next step. Try cases one and seven in the

00:02:45.133 --> 00:02:45.778
live demo.
